-- title:
--   Theorem 4.1, pp. 934–935 — under (4.1)–(4.2) a state-dependent (s, S) feedback policy attains the DP infimum and is optimal
-- statement:
--   Consider the finite-horizon inventory model of Sethi and Cheng with Markov-modulated demand under the standing assumptions of §2:
--   1. the demand state follows a Markov chain $P = (p_{ij})$;
--   2. the demand in period $k$ has density $\varphi_{i,k}$ given state $i$, with mean at most $M$;
--   3. the ordering cost is $c_k(i,u) = K^i_k\delta(u) + c^i_k u$ with $K^i_k, c^i_k \ge 0$;
--   4. the surplus costs $f_k(i,\cdot)$ are convex, nonnegative, zero at $0$, and bounded by $C(1+|x|)$.
--
--   Assume moreover
--   $$K^i_n \ge \bar K^i_{n+1} \equiv \sum_{j=1}^L p_{ij}K^j_{n+1} \ge 0 \quad (4.1), \qquad c^i_n x + F_{n+1}(f_{n+1})(i,x) \to +\infty \ \text{as}\ x\to\infty \quad (4.2)$$
--   for $n \in \langle 0, N-1\rangle$ and $i \in I$.
--
--   Then there are numbers $s^i_n \le S^i_n$, $n \in \langle 0, N-1 \rangle$, $i \in I$, extended reals in $[-\infty, \infty)$ with $S^i_n = -\infty$ only if $s^i_n = -\infty$, such that the $(s,S)$ rule
--   $$\hat u_n(i,x) = (S^i_n - x)\,\delta(s^i_n - x) \qquad (4.8)$$
--   is an optimal feedback policy:
--   1. **(a)** for every $n \in \langle 0, N-1\rangle$, $i$ and $x$, $\hat u_n(i,x) \ge 0$ attains the infimum in the dynamic programming equation (3.2),
--   $$c_n(i,\hat u_n(i,x)) + F_{n+1}(v_{n+1})(i,x+\hat u_n(i,x)) = \inf_{u\ge0}\big\{c_n(i,u) + F_{n+1}(v_{n+1})(i,x+u)\big\};$$
--   2. **(b)** for every initial state $i$ and surplus $x$, the policy generated by (4.8) through (3.3) is admissible and its expected cost equals the value function, $J_0(i,x;\hat U) = v_0(i,x) = \inf_{U} J_0(i,x;U)$, the infimum over all admissible history-dependent policies.
--
--   Thus, with Markov-modulated demand and fixed ordering costs satisfying (4.1), a state-dependent $(s,S)$ policy (order up to $S^i_n$ when the surplus falls below $s^i_n$) is optimal over a finite horizon.
--
--   **Formalization Note**
--   1. Following Remark 4.4, $s^i_n$ and $S^i_n$ are extended reals: $s^i_n = -\infty$ means never order in state $i$ at period $n$. The constraints $S^i_n < +\infty$ and "$S^i_n = -\infty$ only if $s^i_n = -\infty$" make the rule well defined.
--   2. "Optimal feedback policy" is read as both (a), the rule of Theorem 3.1, and (b), optimality of the generated policy by Theorem 3.2.
--   3. The comparison class in (b) is every history-dependent admissible policy, measurable in the observed demands.
--   4. The expectation and the conventions ($u_N = 0$, conditional independence, `Fin L` states) are those of the definition file `SethiChengSS.Finite.Model`.
-- source:
--   Sethi and Cheng, Optimality of (s, S) policies in inventory models with Markovian demand, Oper. Res. 45(6) (1997), DOI 10.1287/opre.45.6.931, pp. 934–935, Theorem 4.1 and (4.8); Remark 4.4

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BertsekasKConvex
import Definitions.Def_SethiChengSS_Finite_KConvexity
import Definitions.Def_SethiChengSS_Finite_Model
open MeasureTheory Filter Topology
open scoped ENNReal

namespace SethiChengSS.Finite

/-- Theorem 4.1 (Sethi–Cheng 1997, pp. 934–935): under the §2 assumptions, (4.1) and (4.2), there
are extended reals `s^i_n ≤ S^i_n` (`n ∈ ⟨0, N − 1⟩`, `i ∈ I`; `S^i_n < +∞`, and `S^i_n = −∞` only
when `s^i_n = −∞`) such that the `(s, S)` rule (4.8) `û_n(i, x) = (S^i_n − x) δ(s^i_n − x)`
(a) attains the infimum of the dynamic programming equation (3.2) at every `n < N`, `i`, `x`, and
(b) generates by (3.3) an admissible policy that is optimal among all admissible
history-dependent policies: `J_0(i, x; Û) = v_0(i, x)`. -/
theorem theorem_4_1 {L : ℕ} (D : Data L) (N : ℕ) (hS : Standing D)
    (h41 : Cond41 D N) (h42 : Cond42 D N) :
    ∃ s S : ℕ → Fin L → EReal,
      (∀ n < N, ∀ i, s n i ≤ S n i ∧ S n i ≠ ⊤ ∧ (s n i ≠ ⊥ → S n i ≠ ⊥)) ∧
      (∀ n < N, ∀ i x,
        0 ≤ orderSS (s n i) (S n i) x ∧
        orderCost D n i (orderSS (s n i) (S n i) x) +
            F D n (dpV D N (n + 1)) i (x + orderSS (s n i) (S n i) x) =
          ⨅ u : {u : ℝ // 0 ≤ u}, (orderCost D n i u + F D n (dpV D N (n + 1)) i (x + u))) ∧
      (∀ i x,
        Admissible N (feedback (fun n i y => orderSS (s n i) (S n i) y) 0 x) ∧
        J D N 0 i x (feedback (fun n i y => orderSS (s n i) (S n i) y) 0 x) =
          value D N 0 i x) := by sorry

end SethiChengSS.Finite
